The GY-521 (MPU-6050) supplies rich continuous data. The mechanical switches supply clean, low-
effort discrete events. Together they create layered awareness without overloading a single sensor or
the microcontroller.
3. Feasibility on Arduino Nano
Short answer: Yes — this combination is well within the Nano’s capabilities and is not
overloaded.
Resource picture:
• GY-521 (MPU-6050): Uses only the I2C bus (A4/A5). Modest sampling rates (20–50 Hz) are
light work.
• Shock / vibration switch: Simple digital switch. One digital pin + INPUT_PULLUP (or module
DO pin). Extremely low overhead.
• Tilt switch: Pure digital input. Negligible cost.
• The Nano still has ample pins, flash, and RAM left for millis-based timing, simple fusion, and
serial output to the ROS 2 pipeline.
Practical guidelines that keep the node reliable:
