2. Add a shock/vibration switch and a tilt switch, handle debouncing, and treat them as clean
digital events.
3. Publish the resulting data in a form that fits the existing ROS 2 sensor pipeline.
4. Explain why continuous IMU data and discrete mechanical switches are complementary rather
than redundant.
5. Design simple reactive behaviors (state change, later sound or motion) driven by orientation or
shock events.
7. Recommended Sequence
Current priority order for the overall project:
5. Clean up and solidify Lesson Sets 01–03 (motion, ranging, arrays) so the foundation is
stable.
6. Complete Lesson Set 04 (this orientation / shock / tilt work) on the Nano + GY-521 + switches.
7. Only then move to the audio module (MAX98357 + MP3 Trigger + speaker path) so sound
reactions have solid sensor events to respond to.
This keeps each layer teachable and prevents the audio work from becoming a distraction while the
core awareness sensors are still being refined.
8. Next Steps After This Introduction
This document is the conceptual and architectural introduction. Subsequent materials in the set will
cover:
• Detailed wiring and pin recommendations for the current Nano + GY-521 + switches setup
• Reference sketches (millis style, matching previous lessons)
• Basic fusion logic and example ROS 2 topic structure
• Suggested experiments and failure modes to explore
This layer of awareness is one of the most important steps toward a droid that feels present and
reactive rather than simply a collection of independent sensors. It remains fully modular, teachable, and
compatible with the long-term goal of a sustainable, reproducible droid platform.
